Potato Make NEWS -- history of user-visible changes.

Version 1.0.1
=============

* Build system and documentation

  - Potato Make now has a GNU-style build system: "./configure",
    "make", "make check", "make install" and "make distcheck" work.
    The modules are byte-compiled when they are installed.
  - The reference documentation is now a Texinfo manual, in
    doc/potato-make.texi.  It replaces reference.md, and adds
    documentation for the (potato lib) module.
  - There is a test suite, using SRFI-64, that is run by "make check".
  - examples/src/makefile.scm is a new example that builds a C program
    from sources that include a file name with a space in it.

* Bug fixes: rules

  - The prerequisites of a target rule are built in the order they are
    listed.  They used to be built in reverse order.
  - A build script that has suffix rules but no target rules no longer
    fails with a "wrong type argument" error.
  - A suffix rule now applies only when its source file exists or can
    be made.  Before, an existing file such as "foo.c" could not be
    used as a prerequisite when some suffix rule, such as ".y" to
    ".c", could in theory have made it.  This broke most uses of the
    --builtins option.
  - A suffix rule with an empty target suffix no longer causes a
    "Stack overflow" error when its source file has no target rule.
  - In suffix rules, $< and $* are correct when the target suffix
    contains more than one period or is empty.
  - The built-in suffix rules look up their makevars when they are
    run, so assignments in the build script, such as (:= CC "clang"),
    affect them.
  - The built-in ".f" to ".o" rule passes "-c" to the compiler,
    instead of ",c".
  - The built-in ".l" to ".c" rule uses LFLAGS instead of LDFLAGS.
  - A recipe procedure that returns a non-zero integer is a failure,
    as documented.
  - With --no-execution, commands made by the recipe helpers are
    printed, not just commands given as literal strings.

* Bug fixes: makevars

  - With --verbosity=3, assigning a makevar with ?= no longer raises
    a "wrong type argument" error.  Neither does dump-makevars.
  - Referencing an empty makevar with a transformer procedure returns
    an empty string instead of raising an error.
  - The procedure returned by $$ and reference-func returns a string
    even when the makevar was lazily assigned a procedure.

* Bug fixes: command line

  - A makevar assignment on the command line may contain more than one
    equals sign, as in CFLAGS=-DDEBUG=1.
  - The option words in MAKEFLAGS, such as "silent", are honored with
    --elevate-environment, not just with --environment.
  - Each call to initialize starts from the default verbosity, instead
    of inheriting it from an earlier call.
  - --help shows the ASCII option as -A, which is what is accepted.
  - --version prints a space between the name and the version.
  - The messages that report the result of a top-level target honor
    --ascii.

* Bug fixes: (potato lib)

  - words, strip, and the procedures built on them treat a quote
    character inside quotes of the other kind as an ordinary
    character, so "it's" is handled properly.
  - file-abspath no longer returns a relative name for a path that has
    ".." at the root, and it removes repeated and trailing slashes.
  - wildcard does not match hidden files unless the pattern begins
    with a period.
  - Importing (potato lib) no longer warns that it overrides the core
    binding sort-list.

Version 1.0
===========

Initial release.
